  "answer_text": "Yes, parathyroid disease **can** cause neck pain, along with a wide range of other symptoms. Here is a detailed breakdown:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd34 Direct Neck-Related Symptoms\n\n- A common symptom of parathyroid disease is a **lump in the neck**, as parathyroid tumors can grow anywhere along the thyroid.\n- In some cases, patients may present with **non-specific neck and shoulder pain**, which can be associated with elevated parathyroid hormone levels and hypercalcemia.\n- Signs and symptoms of parathyroid cancer, in particular, can include weakness, fatigue, and **a lump in the neck**.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83e\uddb4 Bone & Musculoskeletal Pain (Including Neck)\n\n- The most common symptoms of hyperparathyroidism include **chronic fatigue, body aches, bone pain**, memory loss, poor concentration, depression, and headaches.\n- Parathyroid disease can lead to **osteoporosis**, which can cause broken bones and fractures. If one of these fractures happens in the **spine or neck**, it can cause significant pain, and in severe cases, even paralysis.\n- The possibility of parathyroid disease should be considered in the presence of **multiple pathological fractures, generalized bone pain, hypercalcemia**, and a visible neck mass.\n\n---\n\n### \u26a0\ufe0f Other Common Symptoms of Parathyroid Disease\n\nParathyroid disease also frequently leads to **osteoporosis, kidney stones, hypertension, cardiac arrhythmias, and kidney failure**.\n\nAdditional symptoms include:\n- Being much more thirsty than usual, urinating more than usual, constipation, and trouble thinking clearly.\n- **Kidney stones** are a common complication of parathyroid disease, formed when calcium and other minerals combine into crystals \u2014 if these get stuck in the kidney, they can cause pain and swelling.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udccc Why Does This Happen?\n\nParathyroid hormone (PTH) controls calcium levels by acting on the kidneys and bones. When one or more parathyroid glands make too much hormone \u2014 a condition known as **primary hyperparathyroidism** \u2014 calcium is pulled out of the bones and released into the blood, causing a number of problems throughout the body.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udfe5 When to See a Doctor\n\nHyperparathyroidism is a serious disease that becomes more destructive over time and can lead to problems including osteoporosis, high blood pressure, kidney stones, kidney failure, stroke, and cardiac arrhythmias. If you are experiencing unexplained neck pain, a lump in the neck, bone pain, or other symptoms described above, it is important to see a doctor for evaluation, which typically includes blood tests to check calcium and PTH levels.",
